+All significance tests in Section 6 use the primary metric of the respective dataset; secondary metrics, including MAE, RMSE, level-specific sMAPE, peak-load error, and coherence violation, are preserved in the evidence tables.
+
+\subsubsection{3.3. Exact-Hierarchy Labels and Reconciliation}\label{exact-hierarchy-labels-and-reconciliation}
+
+The Ausgrid leaves are customers 134, 104, 255, 141, 228, 147, 55, 131, 21, 82, 58, and 109. Sorting these leaves by \texttt{(postcode,\ customer\ identifier)} and taking consecutive groups of three yields \texttt{region1} = \{82, 147, 255\}, \texttt{region2} = \{228, 109, 131\}, \texttt{region3} = \{134, 141, 55\}, and \texttt{region4} = \{21, 58, 104\}. These \texttt{region*} strings are reproducible synthetic group labels, not administrative-region claims. At every time, each group is the sum of its three leaves and \texttt{system\_total} is the sum of all 12 leaves. The resulting summing matrix \(S\in\{0,1\}^{17\times12}\) has a 12-row identity block, four membership rows, and one all-ones row.
+
+Every model first produces a 17-vector of independent base point forecasts \(\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_t\). The four reported regimes are fully determined as follows:
+
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+\text{Base:}\quad &\tilde y_t=\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_t,\\
+\text{Bottom-up:}\quad &\tilde y_t=S\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_{L,t},\\
+\text{Top-down:}\quad &\tilde y_t=S p\,\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_{T,t},\\
+\text{OLS:}\quad &\tilde y_t=S(S^{\mathsf T}S)^{-1}S^{\mathsf T}\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_t.
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+
+Here \(\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_{L,t}\) contains the 12 base leaf forecasts and \(\hat y^{\mathrm{base}}_{T,t}\) is the base root forecast. The top-down vector is \(p_i=\sum_{t<t_{\mathrm{split}}}y_{i,t}/\sum_{j=1}^{12}\sum_{t<t_{\mathrm{split}}}y_{j,t}\), so only training-prefix leaf energy determines its proportions. The OLS matrix depends only on the fixed \(S\); it does not estimate a residual covariance and is not MinT. No reconciliation uses test targets, nonnegativity clipping, or post-reconciliation refitting. Bottom-up, top-down, and OLS are coherent by construction, whereas Base need not be.
+
+\subsubsection{3.4. Chronological Split, Filters, and Data Visibility}\label{chronological-split-filters-and-data-visibility}
+
+Samples are assigned by forecast-origin index: origins \(t<t_{\mathrm{split}}\) enter the training pool and origins \(t\geq t_{\mathrm{split}}\) enter the test pool. The data are not shuffled. This construction makes fitting-target timestamps and test-target timestamps disjoint, but it does not insert a horizon-length purge between origin sets. At \(h=24\), the last training targets occur 23 retained positions after the first test origin and immediately before the first test target. The same no-purge rule is used between the fitting and validation origin sets. Consequently, the protocol is chronological by origin but is not a strict real-time, leakage-free backtest; all earlier blanket leakage-free wording has been removed.
+
+The filters and fitted quantities have different visibility scopes:
+
+\begin{itemize}
+\tightlist
+\item
+  Per-series means and standard deviations use only values before \(t_{\mathrm{split}}\). The nMAE range uses the common test targets only as an evaluation denominator and never enters training.
+\item
+  After applying the training stride, the final 15\% of training origins by index is the validation set. All declared epochs are run, and the state with the lowest validation MSE is restored; there is no patience-based early termination. Training batches are shuffled within an epoch, but the origin split is unchanged. Test samples are not strided.
+\item
+  OPSD country columns are fixed in code and SimBench profiles are the first eight \texttt{*\_pload} columns in file-header order. Their rowwise validity and hourly-construction rules in Table 1 are applied before splitting but do not rank series by test outcomes.
+\item
+  Ausgrid is an explicit exception to train-only filtering. ``Complete'' means having the maximum accepted day count over the full three-year source window, and the 12 leaves are the highest total-energy customers over that same full window. The filter yields 299 complete customers before selecting 12. Because eligibility and energy ranking see the eventual test period, the hierarchy is a fixed retrospective benchmark, not a train-only selected deployment cohort.
+\item
+  Calendar inputs do not parse the timestamp strings. For target index \(q=t+h\), the code uses \(\sin(2\pi(q\bmod24)/24)\), \(\cos(2\pi(q\bmod24)/24)\), and analogous seven-position sine/cosine terms from \(\lfloor q/24\rfloor\bmod7\). These are positional periodic features. No time-zone conversion, civil weekday anchoring, holiday feature, or daylight-saving correction is applied.
+\end{itemize}
+
+The source profiles record OPSD and SimBench file names, retained row counts, and boundary labels. The Ausgrid configuration records the selected leaves, group memberships, split-independent hierarchy, model list, and reconciliation regimes. These records support reconstruction of the reported arrays but do not remove the no-purge and full-window-selection limitations above.
 
