-\section{8. Limitations}\label{limitations}
+\subsection{8. Limitations}\label{limitations}
 
 \begin{enumerate}
 \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
 \tightlist
 \item
-  \textbf{Claims are confined to day-ahead, pool-level multi-region
-  forecasting.} The single significance-backed superiority result is
-  OPSD 24 h (vs.~MLP and vs.~the no-aggregation ablation). We make no
-  claim at the 1 h horizon --- on OPSD 1 h the method significantly
-  loses to the MLP (Holm p = 0.0348) --- and no claim on SimBench, where
-  both horizons are inseparable from the MLP (24 h: p = 0.084 with the
-  MLP mean ahead; 1 h: p = 1 with means nearly identical).
+  \textbf{The target is a scalar point, not a next-day path.} The single significance-backed superiority result is the OPSD 24-retained-step point task (vs.~MLP and vs.~the no-aggregation ablation). We make no claim at the 1-step horizon, where the method significantly loses to MLP on OPSD (Holm p = 0.0348), or on SimBench, where both horizons are inseparable from MLP (24 steps: p = 0.084 with the MLP mean ahead; 1 step: p = 1 with means nearly identical). The experiments do not support trajectory, probabilistic, or operational day-ahead claims.
+\item
+  \textbf{The method does not transfer to the exact customer/group/system hierarchy as implemented.} Under the common OLS reconciliation, CSA-LoadNet significantly loses to DLinear (Holm \(p<0.001\)), while its contrasts against MLP, TCN, and PatchTST-lite remain unresolved under the primary family. Bottom-up, top-down, and OLS reconciliation are included and the coherent variants satisfy the accounting identities exactly, but reconciliation does not make the proposed forecaster more accurate than the strongest linear baseline. MinT with covariance shrinkage and end-to-end coherence-constrained training remain untested.
+\item
+  \textbf{The weight-form inseparability is a bounded negative.} It holds for pools of 6--17 series on these three public benchmarks under our training budget; larger pools, sparser correlation structure, or genuinely tree-structured populations could separate the weight parameterizations. Our finding licenses skepticism, not a universal impossibility statement.
 \item
-  \textbf{The method does not transfer to the exact
-  customer/region/system hierarchy as implemented.} Under the common OLS
-  reconciliation, CSA-LoadNet significantly loses to DLinear (Holm
-  \(p<0.001\)), while its contrasts against MLP, TCN, and PatchTST-lite
-  remain unresolved under the primary family. Bottom-up, top-down, and
-  OLS reconciliation are included and the coherent variants satisfy the
-  accounting identities exactly, but reconciliation does not make the
-  proposed forecaster more accurate than the strongest linear baseline.
-  MinT with covariance shrinkage and end-to-end coherence-constrained
-  training remain untested.
+  \textbf{Bounded, unequal architecture budgets.} All models use compact configurations with bounded epochs and strided training samples. OPSD and SimBench were trained on CPU and the exact Ausgrid experiment on one RTX 3090. Inputs and evaluation origins are common within a block, but registered parameters range from 338 to 70,597 and epoch caps range from 6 to 20. Absolute errors and rankings could change under equalized compute or architecture-specific tuning.
 \item
-  \textbf{The weight-form inseparability is a bounded negative.} It
-  holds for pools of 6--17 series on these three public benchmarks under
-  our training budget; larger pools, sparser correlation structure, or
-  genuinely tree-structured populations could separate the weight
-  parameterizations. Our finding licenses skepticism, not a universal
-  impossibility statement.
+  \textbf{One retrospectively selected hierarchy and one origin split.} The corrected Ausgrid study removes the leaf/aggregate inconsistency and uses ten seeds for every model, but completeness and top-energy leaf selection use the full three-year window. The resulting 12-leaf benchmark is therefore not a train-only selected cohort. Alternative train-only leaf filters, groupings, rolling origins, and larger customer populations could yield different reconciliation rankings.
 \item
-  \textbf{Bounded training budgets.} All models, baselines included, use
-  compact configurations with bounded epochs and strided training
-  samples. OPSD and SimBench were trained on CPU and the exact Ausgrid
-  experiment on one RTX 3090. Budgets are matched within each comparison
-  block, but absolute errors and even rankings could change under
-  architecture-specific large-scale tuning.
+  \textbf{No exogenous weather inputs.} All models forecast from load history and calendar information alone; weather-aware extensions remain untested on real data in this study, and no claim about them is made.
 \item
-  \textbf{One fixed hierarchy and one temporal split.} The corrected
-  Ausgrid study removes the leaf/aggregate inconsistency and uses ten
-  seeds for every model, but it still tests one deterministic 12-leaf
-  grouping and one chronological split. Alternative geographic
-  groupings, rolling origins, and larger customer populations could
-  yield different reconciliation rankings.
+  \textbf{Weather-year sensitivity is untested.} The OPSD conclusions rest on one forecast-origin split of the 2015--2018 period, whose test targets run from October 2017 through December 2018. Because all inputs are load history and positional periodic features, an unusually mild or extreme weather-year could shift both absolute errors and the size of the cross-series aggregation benefit. The findings have not been replicated across alternative multi-year split positions, and we make no claim of weather-year robustness.
 \item
-  \textbf{No exogenous weather inputs.} All models forecast from load
-  history and calendar information alone; weather-aware extensions
-  remain untested on real data in this study, and no claim about them is
-  made.
+  \textbf{Timestamp and missingness handling is index-based.} Complete-case filtering compresses 41 missing OPSD timestamps into seven internal gaps, while the model treats consecutive retained rows as consecutive hours. SimBench and Ausgrid use timezone-naive constructed labels, and the calendar channel uses index phase rather than localized civil time. The implementation does not handle daylight-saving transitions, holidays, or elapsed-time gaps. Consequently, the OPSD \(h=24\) task is not literally 24 elapsed hours for origins whose windows or targets cross a removed gap.
 \item
-  \textbf{Weather-year sensitivity is untested.} The OPSD conclusions
-  rest on a single chronological split of the 2015--2018 period, whose
-  test segment (October 2017 through December 2018, Table 1) spans
-  essentially one weather-year. Because all inputs are load history and
-  calendar features, an unusually mild or extreme weather-year could
-  shift both absolute errors and the size of the cross-series
-  aggregation benefit --- synchronized weather regimes are precisely the
-  shared structure we conjecture the pooled context exploits (Section
-  7.2). The findings have not been replicated across alternative
-  multi-year split positions, and we make no claim of weather-year
-  robustness.
+  \textbf{The origin split is not purged by the horizon.} Training and test target timestamps are disjoint, but the last \(h=24\) training labels occur after the earliest test origins. Validation uses the same no-purge convention. A strict deployment-style rerun would separate fitting, validation, and test origins by at least the forecast horizon before making a leakage-free claim. This rerun has not been performed.
 \end{enumerate}
 
